Free-base robustness acceptance
-------------------------------
artifacts/freebase-robustness.json
SHA-256: ac90d750c89d4438f294059a782da61fdae7f5a07bcc7e5b2f5f8165345357bf
Schema 3; result: FAIL. Critical trials passed 10/10. Broad randomized trials
passed 16/20 (Wilson lower 95% 0.583983). The independent single-support
holdout passed only 14/40: right 9/20 (Wilson 0.258198), left 5/20 (Wilson
0.111862), all below the required 0.80. Across all broad and holdout randomized
trials, 24 exceeded the 9 N*s landing-impulse limit and 10 exceeded the 0.065 m
lateral CoM-to-stance limit; 8 holdout trials ended in a real fall. Categories
overlap.

Rejected controller experiments
-------------------------------
No video-specific or seed-fitted controller was merged. A high-authority
jumping-leg mapping met the source clip but fell on non-periodic inputs; a
bounded robust mapping could not meet the required leg amplitude. Squat
planners that met 4/4 nominal channels had narrow/history-dependent stability
and failed clean entry or holdout checks. Force/CoP landing overlays and a
fresh closed-loop shift law failed independent or even nominal trials. The
remaining one-leg defect requires a coupled constrained shift/lift trajectory
planner with predictive braking and a terminal-set controller.

Interpretation and hardware boundary
------------------------------------
The free scene has neq=0 and a real base_free joint. The live simulator is
controlled only through the canonical 20 motor targets; the acceptance tools
also prove one HumanoidSimulation/MjData instance across replay and settling.
No-fall is not equivalent to passing contact safety or pose fidelity.

The MuJoCo inertial/contact model remains provisional. The legacy WebSocket is
a 10 Hz command-only output: there is no real encoder/IMU/foot-load feedback,
execution acknowledgement, onboard watchdog or hardware E-stop in this repo.
These simulation results are not a physical-robot safety certificate.
